  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was soft across both locations\u2014down 32% in total revenue\u2014but this is a capacity story, not a performance story. Port Washington ran just 2 active groomers ($428/groomer vs. a $466 baseline), while Hicksville ran 3 ($345/groomer vs. a $416 baseline). Both stores underperformed their productivity benchmarks because you didn't have enough bodies on the floor. The good news: month-to-date, Port Washington is tracking +1.5% above DOW-adjusted expectation, and Hicksville is crushing it at +18.4%\u2014that's real operational outperformance, not calendar luck. Year-to-date Port Washington is up 44% and Hicksville is delivering strong growth in its first full year. You should be proud of both stores' underlying health.\n\nWhat needs attention: add-on attach rates dropped meaningfully at both locations (Port Washington from 58% to 50%, Hicksville from 65% to 50%), signaling upsell discipline slipped yesterday. This is fixable with front-line coaching. Retail also diverged\u2014down 31% at Port Washington and 34% at Hicksville\u2014likely a function of low transaction volume, but monitor whether basket size is also softening. On inventory, you have three treats running negative stock (Shanti's Medical Journey, X-Small Treat Table, WGB Bully Sticks); reorder immediately to avoid lost sales.\n\nCapacity & coverage: Today you have 1 groomer and 15 bather hours scheduled at Port Washington, tomorrow 5 groomers and 15 bather hours; next 7 days across both stores you're at 53 groomer-days combined (35 Port Washington, 18 Hicksville) with 83 bather hours, which is light and will need monitoring through Labor Day week."
